e they're catchy, rhyme, or "feel right"—not because someone verified them scientifically first. "Blind as a bat" has alliteration (the repeated "b" sound), which makes it memorable and fun to say, aiding its spread regardless of accuracy. Once a phrase enters common usage, it tends to stick around due to cultural inertia, even after science corrects the underlying assumption. So the existence of the phrase tells us about **historical human perception and language patterns**, not about bat biology. It's a great example of how figurative language can outlive and outpace the facts it was originally based on.
