g you've ever learned. **A little uncertainty is actually normal and healthy** Even experts hold beliefs that later turn out to be incomplete or wrong—that's just how learning works, in science and everyday life. It doesn't mean you can't trust anything; it just means knowledge is provisional and updates over time, and that's okay. So no, you don't need to unravel your entire mental library of animal facts. Just consider it a fun/humbling reminder that a few "well-known" facts are actually myths, and if one of those comes up again, you can check rather than assume. That's it—no existential crisis required.
