11:36:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Good question, but that's a bit of a logical leap—being *known for* something doesn't mean every individual needs it exclusively or lacks alternatives. Quick example: Cheetahs are "the animal known for speed," but that doesn't mean every cheetah is incapable of walking, standing still, or using stealth sometimes. Being famous for one trait doesn't erase other traits or make them nonfunctional. Same logic with bats: - **~1,400 bat species exist.** Echolocation is common among many microbat species, but not universal across the entire order. - **Megabats (~200 species)**, like flying foxes, are still "bats" by classification, but most don't echolocate at all and rely on vision/smell. - Even among echolocating microbats, echolocation being their **signature, most famous trait** doesn't mean their eyes are vestigial or non-functional—it just means it's the adaptation that makes them scientifically/culturally distinctive. So "bats = echolocation" is accurate as a **defining, well-known trait of the group**, similar to "bees = pollination" or "elephants = trunks"—but that doesn't mean literally every individual bat has zero use for eyes, any more than every bee's entire biology revolves solely around pollination.
